# Tripo usage

Tripo generates **3D models only**. Never use it for 2D work.

Concept art, kit references, and any other image come from the image generation
tool, which costs no Tripo credits. Never call `tripo generate text-to-image`,
`image-to-image`, `image-to-multiview`, `edit-multiview`, or `image-to-splat`,
and never pass `--model seedream_v4` or any other image model.

Auth: the working key is the `tsk_` value in `.env`. The `tcli_` one named
`TRIPO_API_KEY` returns 401. Set `$env:TRIPO_API_KEY` from the `tsk_` value.

## Spend the minimum per asset

One `image_to_model` call is 50 credits and already returns a finished GLB with
2K JPEG PBR textures. Everything after that is an extra billed task.

**Do not run `convert`** for texture resizing. It is a separate 10-credit task
(non-default options bill at the "complex convert" tier) that re-bakes nothing —
it only repacks the same mesh with smaller images. Unreal applies a maximum
texture size at import for free, so ship the untouched generator GLB.

Only convert when the *format* itself is required, such as FBX for a pipeline
that cannot read glTF. Resolution alone is never a reason.

```powershell
# Good — one billed task, 50 credits
tripo make art/enemies/equation/<kit>/<concept>.png `
  --model tripo-p1 -p face_limit=800 -p texture=true -p pbr=true `
  -p texture_quality=standard -p texture_alignment=original_image -p auto_size=false `
  -o art/tripo-out --json --yes --no-open

# Bad — 60 credits for textures Unreal would have downsized for nothing
tripo make ... --then convert:format=GLTF,texture_size=512
```

Other budget rules: `--model tripo-p1` for kit parts (`face_limit` 50–20000);
`texture_quality=standard` (`detailed` costs time and shows nothing at these
sizes); `-n` candidates multiply the 50-credit charge, so re-roll with
`tripo redo` only when a result is unusable.

Check `tripo balance` before a batch and report the spend afterwards. Verify any
result locally with `node art/glb-inspect.mjs <file.glb>` rather than paying for
another task to inspect it.

## Orientation

Tripo aligns a result to the concept image's implied camera, not to any axis, so
a 3/4 hero shot yields a mesh sitting on a diagonal. That is not cosmetic. Parts
are fitted by scaling their bounding box, and a diagonal rod's box was fifteen
times the volume it needed, so it was sized wrong however good its sockets were;
socket seeding also finds end faces by slicing along world axes, which on a
tilted part slices through its middle.

So **render kit concepts as a flat elevation, straight down one axis**, with the
part's mount axis vertical in frame and no perspective tilt. A 3/4 view is for
presentation, never for something that will be assembled procedurally.

Assume it still comes back crooked and check. This is a pipeline step, not a
judgement call:

```powershell
node art/align-audit.mjs                     # tilt per mesh, before
blender --background --python art/enemies/equation/cold-iron-kit/models/align-assets.py --
node art/align-audit.mjs <aligned-dir>       # every mesh should read 0.0 deg
```

`align-assets.py` rotates geometry onto the family's canonical axes and bakes it
in, because Unreal only ever applies a rigid rotation on top and cannot undo a
baked tilt. Re-importing replaces the assets, which discards their sockets, so
re-seed afterwards — never align a kit that has hand-corrected sockets without
expecting to redo them.
